<onlyinclude>'''Socionics''' is a personality and interpersonal-relations typology developed by the Lithuanian researcher Aušra Augustinavičiūtė in the former Soviet Union. It combines ideas from Carl Jung's psychological types with Antoni Kępiński's [[Information metabolism/Simple|information metabolism]] and proposes sixteen types of information processing.

== What the theory proposes ==
Socionics assigns different roles to eight proposed information-processing functions. Model A, one of its central models, organizes them in different positions for each type. Advocates use these patterns to describe preferred ways of attending to information and to predict how people with different types may interact.

For example, the theory may assign two people to a relationship pattern based on their supposed types. Whether those people actually communicate effectively is an empirical question, not something established by assigning the labels.

== Scientific status ==
Socionics is a historical and theoretical personality system. Descriptions of its categories and internal rules do not establish that those categories exist as discrete psychological kinds or that its interpersonal predictions are reliable. Personality research also uses continuous trait measurements rather than assigning everyone to one of sixteen fixed types.

Socionics should not be treated as a validated diagnostic instrument or as a basis for consequential employment, clinical or relationship decisions without evidence supporting that particular use.

